{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 32,
  "title": "Work",
  "description": "Name the jobs, and say what you do.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Every one of these jobs ends in -o and is masculine, and none of them takes an article. That is the rule: a profession is what you are, so it goes straight after the verb.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The jobs",
        "body": "El abogado, el cocinero, el enfermero, el periodista, el policía, el programador, el diseñador, el veterinario, el taxista y el campesino are all masculine. Most of them end in -ero or -ista, and the ending makes the job: el programador programs, el taxista drives a taxi.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el abogado",
            "en": "the lawyer"
          },
          {
            "es": "el enfermero",
            "en": "the nurse"
          },
          {
            "es": "el programador",
            "en": "the programmer"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "No article in between",
        "body": "A profession goes straight after the verb, with no article: soy abogado, ella es enfermera. The feminine is easy: change the -o to -a, so el abogado becomes la abogada. When you use the job with an article, it is el abogado que trabaja aquí, the lawyer who works here.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "soy periodista",
            "en": "I am a journalist"
          },
          {
            "es": "ella es programista",
            "en": "she is a programmer"
          },
          {
            "es": "es un trabajo interesante",
            "en": "it is an interesting job"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
